I've never used Volatility before, and lots of CyberDefenders labs seem to focus on it, so let's dive in and see what it's all about.
First, I had to actually install Volatility on my Kali VM. That was as easy as:
git clone https://github.com/volatilityfoundation/volatility3.git
sudo apt-get install requirements.txt


Identifying the name of the malicious process helps in understanding the nature of the attack. What is the name of the malicious process?
The lab told us this is from a Windows machine, so let's run:
python3 vol.py -f [file] windows.pslist
Well, that gave us a lot of information, but not necessarily anything fishy catching my eye immediately. Let's try:
python3 vol.py -f [file] windows.malware.malfind.Malfind
